IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hra-dates`
IMPORT `hra-rules`

-- Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) -- the questions of the INDIVIDUAL whose health information it is: the patient, the
-- injured person or the claimant who wants their own records, wants them corrected, wants to know who may collect, use
-- or disclose them, or wants to complain. "You" in every @desc is that individual. A person acting for them (an
-- authorised representative, a person they named in a written authority, the legal representative of their estate) asks
-- the same questions about them. The Act's terms with plain glosses; the organisation's, the Health Complaints
-- Commissioner's and the Tribunal's judgments are inputs. Each question takes only plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates,
-- a choice from a list); the three duties with deadlines are DEONTIC rules run inside, from hra-rules.l4.
--
-- Where a provision is used by one question only, its words are applied here, with the quotation; the shared
-- definitions and the regulative rules are in hra-rules.l4.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Health Records
-- Act 2001, Version No. 050 (1 May 2026), not the authorised version. (c) State of Victoria; published with the
-- authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced except in accordance with the
-- Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Records Act 2001 -- the individual's questions`

§§ `What the Act covers`

@export Is this "health information" about me under the Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 3? It covers information or an opinion about your physical, mental or psychological health, a disability, your wishes about future health services, or a health service to you, and other personal information collected in providing a health service, about organ or tissue donation, or predictive genetic information, unless prescribed as exempt.
GIVEN `is it about my health or a health service to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is information or an opinion about your physical, mental or psychological health (at any time), a disability you have or had, your expressed wishes about future health services, or a health service provided or to be provided to you
      `was it collected in providing a health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is other personal information collected to provide, or in providing, a health service (for example your address on a hospital file)
      `is it about donating my organs or tissue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is personal information collected in connection with your donation, or intended donation, of body parts, organs or body substances
      `is it predictive genetic information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is genetic information about you in a form that is or could be predictive of the health of you or your descendants
      `can I be identified from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your identity is apparent, or can reasonably be worked out, from the information or opinion (true or not, recorded or not)
      `have I been dead more than 30 years` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person the information is about has been dead for more than 30 years (answer no while you are alive)
      `is it prescribed as exempt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether regulations prescribe this information, or its class, as exempt health information (the regulations are not deposited here; usually no)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is it my health information` MEANS
    `s 3 -- it is health information` `is it about my health or a health service to me` `was it collected in providing a health service` `is it about donating my organs or tissue` `is it predictive genetic information` `can I be identified from it` `have I been dead more than 30 years` `is it prescribed as exempt`

-- ss 10, 11: the Act applies to the bodies listed in s 10(1) and to any natural person, body corporate, partnership,
-- trust or unincorporated body "that is a health service provider or collects, holds or uses health information".
-- ss 10(5), 11(4): "nothing in this Act applies to an agency within the meaning of the Privacy Act 1988 of the
-- Commonwealth". s 13: "Nothing in this Act or in any HPP applies" to an individual handling health information "only for
-- the purposes of ... his or her personal, family or household affairs". s 14: courts and tribunals in their judicial
-- functions, and their registries. s 14A: Royal Commissions, Boards of Inquiry and Formal Reviews. s 15: generally
-- available publications, library, gallery and museum material, public records open under the Public Records Act 1973,
-- and archives, unless obtained by a contravention the organisation knows of. The flat "nothing ... applies": JUST
-- FALSE. A person or body that is not a health service provider and neither collects, holds nor uses health
-- information: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Does the Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) apply to the person or body that holds or handles my health information (ss 10 to 15)? It applies to Victorian public bodies and to private persons and bodies that provide a health service in Victoria or collect, hold or use health information, but not to Commonwealth agencies, personal or household affairs, courts acting judicially, Royal Commissions, or published material.
GIVEN `is it a health service provider or does it handle health information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person or body provides a health service in Victoria, or collects, holds or uses health information
      `is it a Commonwealth agency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is an agency within the meaning of the Commonwealth Privacy Act 1988 (a Commonwealth department or authority); the Commonwealth Act covers it instead
      `is it an individual handling it only for personal or family affairs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is an individual who handles the information only for their own personal, family or household affairs
      `is it a court or tribunal acting judicially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a court or tribunal (or its registry or office holder) handling the information in its judicial or quasi-judicial functions
      `is it a Royal Commission or inquiry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a Royal Commission, Board of Inquiry or Formal Review handling the information for its functions
      `is the information already published` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information is in a generally available publication, a library, gallery or museum collection, a public record open to inspection, or archives, or is otherwise generally available to the public (answer no if it got there by a breach of the Act that the body knows of)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the Act apply to them` MEANS
    IF `is it a Commonwealth agency` OR `is it an individual handling it only for personal or family affairs` OR `is it a court or tribunal acting judicially` OR `is it a Royal Commission or inquiry` OR `is the information already published`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `is it a health service provider or does it handle health information`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part 5 and HPP 6 -- my right of access`

DECLARE `My access` IS ONE OF
    `access in the way I ask under s 28`
    `an accurate summary, or more if the organisation agrees`
    `access only under the Freedom of Information Act 1982`
    `no access under this Act`

-- s 25(1) "an individual has a right of access, in accordance with this Part and HPP 6, to health information relating
-- to the individual held by a health service provider or any other organisation." (2) all information collected on or
-- after the commencement of s 25 (1 July 2002, endnote 1); (3) "also applies to" eight kinds collected before. s 28(4):
-- for the s 25(3) kinds, access only in a s 28 way "with the agreement of the organisation" or "in the absence of such an
-- agreement, by receiving an accurate summary"; s 29(2) the same. s 16: for a document of an agency or a Minister under
-- the Freedom of Information Act 1982, "nothing in Part 5 ... applies" and access "can only be required" under that Act.
-- s 17(2): nothing in Part 5 applies to a news medium's information for its news activities until published. s 14F(2):
-- nothing in Part 5 applies to the Secretary's Electronic Patient Health Information Sharing System. s 96: the Act does
-- not apply to a document subject to legal professional privilege or client legal privilege. Not held by the
-- organisation: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*. Collected before 1 July 2002 and of none of the s 25(3) kinds:
-- NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (s 25(2), (3) set a scope with no "only"; ruling (c) of 2026-11-05).
@export Do I have a right of access to my health information held by this organisation, and in what form (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 25, 28(4), 16, 17, 96)? You have a right of access to health information about you collected from 1 July 2002; for older records of listed kinds you are entitled to an accurate summary unless the organisation agrees to more. Victorian government agencies give access under the Freedom of Information Act 1982 instead.
GIVEN `does the organisation hold it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has the document containing the information in its possession or control, alone or jointly, wherever it is (s 5)
      `was it collected on or after 1 July 2002` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information was collected on or after 1 July 2002, when the right of access began
      `is it an older record of a listed kind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If collected before 1 July 2002: whether it is a health history, examination findings, investigation results, a diagnosis, a treatment or care plan, a record of action taken or services given, donation information, or predictive genetic information about you
      `is it an FOI agency or Minister's document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a document of a Victorian government agency, or an official document of a Minister, under the Freedom of Information Act 1982 (public hospitals, departments and councils are usually agencies)
      `is it a news medium's unpublished information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is held by a news medium for its news activities and has not been published
      `is it in the Electronic Patient Health Information Sharing System` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is held by the Secretary to the Department of Health in the Electronic Patient Health Information Sharing System
      `is it legally privileged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is subject to legal professional privilege or client legal privilege
GIVETH A MAYBE `My access`
`Do I have a right of access` MEANS
    IF NOT `does the organisation hold it`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `is it legally privileged` OR `is it a news medium's unpublished information` OR `is it in the Electronic Patient Health Information Sharing System`
         THEN JUST `no access under this Act`
         ELSE IF `is it an FOI agency or Minister's document`
              THEN JUST `access only under the Freedom of Information Act 1982`
              ELSE IF `was it collected on or after 1 July 2002`
                   THEN JUST `access in the way I ask under s 28`
                   ELSE IF `is it an older record of a listed kind`
                        THEN JUST `an accurate summary, or more if the organisation agrees`
                        ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Access decision` IS ONE OF
    `the organisation must give me access`
    `the organisation must not give me access: a serious threat to life or health (s 26)`
    `the organisation must not give me access: given in confidence (s 27)`
    `the organisation may refuse access on another HPP 6.1 ground`
    `the organisation may refuse my authorised representative (s 85(5))`
    `the organisation may give me an explanation instead (HPP 6.2)`

DECLARE `Other refusal ground` IS ONE OF
    `no other ground`
    `unreasonable impact on other people's privacy (HPP 6.1(b))`
    `existing legal proceedings and not discoverable, or privileged (HPP 6.1(c))`
    `would reveal its intentions in negotiations with me (HPP 6.1(d))`
    `access would be unlawful (HPP 6.1(f))`
    `denial required or authorised by law (HPP 6.1(g))`
    `would prejudice an investigation of unlawful activity (HPP 6.1(h))`
    `would prejudice a law enforcement function (HPP 6.1(i))`
    `a law enforcement agency asked, for national security (HPP 6.1(j))`
    `a repeat of an unsuccessful request with no reasonable grounds (HPP 6.1(k))`
    `an unreasonable repeat of a request already met (HPP 6.1(l))`

-- s 26: "An organisation must not give an individual access ... if the organisation believes on reasonable grounds,
-- having regard to the guidelines ..., that the provision of the health information ... would pose a serious threat to
-- the life or health of the individual or any other person." s 27(1), (2): "must not give ... access ... if the health
-- information is subject to confidentiality", that is, "given in confidence to the person who recorded the health
-- information by a person other than (a) the individual; or (b) a health service provider in the course of ... the
-- provision of health services to the individual -- with a request that the information not be communicated"; (3) the
-- giver "may consent". HPP 6.1: "it must provide the individual with access ... unless" one of (a) to (l); (a) is s 26
-- with the guidelines, (e) is s 27. HPP 6.2: for "evaluative information generated within the organisation in connection
-- with a commercially sensitive decision-making process, the organisation may give the individual an explanation ...
-- rather than access". s 85(5): "An organisation may refuse a request by an authorised representative ... if the
-- organisation reasonably believes that access by the authorised representative may prejudice the health or safety of
-- the individual." HPP 6.4: nothing compels a refusal except ss 26, 27. No right of access: NOTHING-kind *limited by its
-- own wording*. The "unless" met: the organisation may refuse (fork F3).
@export Must the organisation give me access to my health information, or may or must it refuse (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 26, 27, 85(5), HPP 6.1, 6.2)? It must give access unless a ground applies: it must refuse where it reasonably believes access would seriously threaten anyone's life or health, or where a third party gave the information in confidence; it may refuse on the other HPP 6.1 grounds.
GIVEN `do I have a right of access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a right of access to the information under Part 5 (the right of access question answers this)
      `does it reasonably believe access would seriously threaten someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation believes on reasonable grounds, having regard to any Health Complaints Commissioner guidelines, that giving you the information would pose a serious threat to your life or health or anyone else's
      `was it given in confidence by a third party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information was given in confidence to the person who recorded it, by someone other than you or a health service provider treating you, with a request that it not be passed on to you
      `has the third party consented to my being told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether the person who gave it in confidence has consented to it being communicated to you
      `which other ground applies` IS AN `Other refusal ground` @desc Whether one of the other HPP 6.1 grounds applies, and which (choose the "no ... ground" value if none)
      `is it evaluative commercially sensitive information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether access would reveal evaluative information generated within the organisation in connection with a commercially sensitive decision-making process (for example an insurer's internal assessment)
      `is my authorised representative asking and may prejudice me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request is made by your authorised representative and the organisation reasonably believes access by that representative may prejudice your health or safety
GIVETH A MAYBE `Access decision`
`Must I be given access` MEANS
    IF NOT `do I have a right of access`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `does it reasonably believe access would seriously threaten someone`
         THEN JUST `the organisation must not give me access: a serious threat to life or health (s 26)`
         ELSE IF `was it given in confidence by a third party` AND (NOT `has the third party consented to my being told`)
              THEN JUST `the organisation must not give me access: given in confidence (s 27)`
              ELSE IF NOT (`which other ground applies` EQUALS `no other ground`)
                   THEN JUST `the organisation may refuse access on another HPP 6.1 ground`
                   ELSE IF `is my authorised representative asking and may prejudice me`
                        THEN JUST `the organisation may refuse my authorised representative (s 85(5))`
                        ELSE IF `is it evaluative commercially sensitive information`
                             THEN JUST `the organisation may give me an explanation instead (HPP 6.2)`
                             ELSE JUST `the organisation must give me access`

-- s 28(1)(c): access "by viewing the health information and, if it is held by a health service provider, having its
-- content explained". (2): an organisation that is not a health service provider "may agree to provide an explanation".
-- The "if": not a health service provider and no agreement, JUST FALSE.
@export If I view my health information, must its content be explained to me (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 28(1)(c), (2))? A health service provider must have it explained (by itself if suitably qualified and willing, or by a suitably qualified Victorian practitioner); any other organisation may agree to.
GIVEN `is the organisation a health service provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation holding it is a health service provider (it provides a health service in Victoria: assessment, diagnosis, treatment, disability, palliative or aged care, dispensing)
      `has the organisation agreed to explain it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is not a health service provider (for example an insurer or employer): whether it has agreed to provide an explanation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will the content be explained to me` MEANS
    IF `is the organisation a health service provider` OR `has the organisation agreed to explain it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 30(1): "If an individual -- (a) has a right of access; and (b) has signed a written authority for access to be
-- provided to a person named in the authority -- the person so named must be given access". (2) a written authority
-- "includes a supportive attorney appointment". s 31: the organisation may first require evidence of identity and of the
-- authority. The "if": JUST FALSE.
@export Must the organisation give access to the person I named in a signed written authority (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 30)? Yes, if you have a right of access and signed a written authority naming them (for example your lawyer); the organisation may first ask for evidence of their identity and of the authority (s 31).
GIVEN `do I have a right of access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a right of access to the information under Part 5
      `have I signed a written authority naming them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have signed a written authority for access to be given to that named person (a supportive attorney appointment counts)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the person I authorised be given access` MEANS
    IF `do I have a right of access` AND `have I signed a written authority naming them`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Who is acting` IS ONE OF
    `me personally`
    `my supportive attorney, support person, supportive administrator or supportive guardian`
    `my guardian`
    `my attorney under an enduring power of attorney`
    `my medical treatment decision maker`
    `my administrator`
    `my parent`
    `another person empowered by law to act for me`
    `the legal representative of my estate`
    `someone else`

-- s 85(2): the power to request access or correction, or the right of access, "may be exercised -- (a) by -- (i) the
-- individual personally, except if the individual is a child who is incapable of making the request; or (ii) a supportive
-- attorney ...; or (iii) a support person ...; or (iv) a supportive administrator ...; or (v) a supportive guardian ...;
-- and (b) by an authorised representative of the individual if -- (i) the individual is incapable of making the request
-- or exercising the right of access; and (ii) the health information to be accessed is reasonably necessary for the
-- lawful performance of functions or duties or exercise of powers in respect of the individual by the authorised
-- representative." (3) incapable "by reason of age, injury, disease, senility, illness, disability, physical impairment
-- or mental disorder" of understanding or communicating, despite reasonable assistance. (4) a representative "must not
-- ... request access to, or the correction of, health information if the authorised representative knows or believes
-- that the ... request does not accord with the wishes expressed" before incapacity: "void". (6) authorised
-- representative: guardian, enduring attorney, medical treatment decision maker, administrator, "a parent of an
-- individual, if the individual is a child", or a person "otherwise empowered under law" -- "except to the extent that
-- acting as an authorised representative ... is inconsistent with an order made by a court or tribunal". s 95(1), (2): for
-- a person dead 30 years or less, their rights are exercisable "by a legal representative" (an executor with probate, or
-- an administrator). "except", "if", "must not ... void": JUST FALSE. A person outside the lists: NOTHING-kind *limited by
-- its own wording*.
@export Can this person exercise my right of access to my health information, or ask for its correction, for me (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 85, 95)? You may yourself (unless you are a child unable to make the request); a supportive attorney or support person may; an authorised representative (guardian, enduring attorney, medical treatment decision maker, administrator, or a parent of a child) may only if you are incapable and the information is reasonably necessary for their role; after death, the executor or administrator of your estate.
GIVEN `who is acting` IS A `Who is acting` @desc Who would make the request or exercise the right: you, your supportive attorney or support person (or supportive administrator or guardian), your guardian, enduring attorney, medical treatment decision maker, administrator, your parent, another person empowered by law, the executor (with probate) or administrator of your estate, or someone else
      `am I a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the person the information is about) are under 18
      `am I incapable of making the request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of age, injury, disease, illness, disability, impairment or mental disorder, you cannot understand the general nature and effect of the request, or cannot communicate it, even with reasonable help
      `is it reasonably necessary for their role` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a representative: whether the information is reasonably necessary for them to lawfully perform their functions or duties, or exercise their powers, for you
      `would it go against my expressed wishes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a representative: whether they know or believe the request does not accord with wishes you expressed, and did not change or withdraw, before you became incapable
      `would it be inconsistent with a court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a representative: whether acting for you would be inconsistent with an order of a court or tribunal
      `have I died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the person the information is about) have died
      `have I been dead more than 30 years` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you have died: whether it was more than 30 years ago
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this person act for me` MEANS
    IF `have I died`
    THEN IF `have I been dead more than 30 years`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE CONSIDER `who is acting`
              WHEN `the legal representative of my estate` THEN JUST TRUE
              OTHERWISE NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `who is acting`
         WHEN `me personally` THEN IF `am I a child` AND `am I incapable of making the request` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE
         WHEN `my supportive attorney, support person, supportive administrator or supportive guardian` THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN `my guardian` THEN `as an authorised representative`
         WHEN `my attorney under an enduring power of attorney` THEN `as an authorised representative`
         WHEN `my medical treatment decision maker` THEN `as an authorised representative`
         WHEN `my administrator` THEN `as an authorised representative`
         WHEN `another person empowered by law to act for me` THEN `as an authorised representative`
         WHEN `my parent` THEN IF `am I a child` THEN `as an authorised representative` ELSE NOTHING
         OTHERWISE NOTHING
    WHERE
        `as an authorised representative` MEANS
            IF `would it be inconsistent with a court order`
            THEN JUST FALSE
            ELSE IF `am I incapable of making the request` AND `is it reasonably necessary for their role` AND (NOT `would it go against my expressed wishes`)
                 THEN JUST TRUE
                 ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 34 -- by when the organisation must answer`

-- s 34(1): "This section applies to a request made under section 33, other than an oral request which the organisation
-- has asked the individual to make in writing." s 33(4)(b): the organisation then "need not take any further action in
-- respect of the oral request". No period runs on that oral request: NOTHING (a date cannot say no; the flat "need not"
-- is recorded here). A written request starts its own 45 days.
@export By when must the organisation answer my request for access to my health information (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 34(2))? Within 45 days after it receives the request it must give you a written reason for refusal, a written notice of the fee, or access. If you asked orally and it asked you to put the request in writing, no time runs until you do.
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your request for access (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-11-09)
      `did it ask me to put an oral request in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked orally and the organisation asked you to make the request in writing (s 33(4))
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must the organisation answer my access request` MEANS
    IF `did it ask me to put an oral request in writing`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`s 34(2) -- the last day to answer` `the day it received my request`)

@export By when must the organisation give me access after I pay its fee (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 34(2)(b)(ii))? Within 7 days after you pay the fee, or 45 days after it received your request, whichever is later.
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your request for access (ISO 8601)
      `the day I paid the fee` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee stated in the organisation's notice (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I be given access after paying` MEANS
    `s 34(2)(b)(ii) -- the last day to give access` `the day it received my request` `the day I paid the fee`

@export Has the organisation answered my access request in time (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 34(2), 35(a))? It must give a written reason for refusal, a written fee notice, or access within 45 days after receiving the request; if it does not, it is taken to have refused access, and you may complain to the Health Complaints Commissioner. Answers done in time, not done in time, or still open.
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your written request (ISO 8601)
      `has it answered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has given you a written reason for refusal, a written fee notice, or access
      `the day it answered` IS A DATE @desc If it has: the day it did (ISO 8601; any date if it has not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `What became of the duty`
`Has my access request been answered in time` MEANS
    `what became of the organisation's duty` (`s 34(2) -- the organisation must answer my access request` (`the days from` `the day it received my request` `to` (`s 34(2) -- the last day to answer` `the day it received my request`))) `answer my access request` `has it answered` (`the days from` `the day it received my request` `to` `the day it answered`) (`the days from` `the day it received my request` `to` `today`)

@export Has the organisation given me access in time after I paid its fee (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 34(2)(b)(ii), 35(a))? Access is due within 7 days after payment or 45 days after the request, whichever is later; if it is late, the organisation is taken to have refused access. Answers done in time, not done in time, or still open.
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your request (ISO 8601)
      `the day I paid the fee` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee (ISO 8601)
      `have I been given access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has given you access
      `the day I was given access` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day (ISO 8601; any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `What became of the duty`
`Have I been given access in time after paying` MEANS
    `what became of the organisation's duty` (`s 34(2)(b)(ii) -- the organisation must give me access after the fee is paid` (`the days from` `the day it received my request` `to` (`s 34(2)(b)(ii) -- the last day to give access` `the day it received my request` `the day I paid the fee`))) `give me access after the fee is paid` `have I been given access` (`the days from` `the day it received my request` `to` `the day I was given access`) (`the days from` `the day it received my request` `to` `today`)

-- s 35: "An organisation is taken to have refused access to the health information if -- (a) the organisation fails to
-- comply with a requirement of section 29 or 34; or (b) in the case of a notice given ... under section 29(1)(a) or
-- 29(1)(d)(i) -- (i) the notice does not specify a date, time or place that, in all the circumstances, is reasonable; or
-- (ii) the organisation unreasonably fails to allow inspection ..., or to give a reasonable explanation, in accordance
-- with the notice; or (c) in the case of a notice ... under section 29(1)(d)(ii), the health service provider specified
-- in the notice unreasonably fails -- (i) to make an arrangement ... or (ii) to comply with an arrangement so made." The
-- "if" unmet: JUST FALSE (the organisation is not taken to have refused; it may still have refused in fact).
@export Is the organisation taken to have refused me access (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 35)? It is if it misses a requirement of s 29 or s 34 (such as the 45-day answer), names an unreasonable date, time or place to inspect or have the information explained, or unreasonably fails to let you inspect or to explain as its notice said. A refusal can be the subject of a complaint.
GIVEN `has it missed a requirement of s 29 or s 34` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has failed to comply with s 29 (how access is given) or s 34 (the 45-day answer, or access after the fee); the in-time questions answer the deadlines
      `was the date, time or place unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether its written notice for inspecting or explaining the information named a date, time or place that was not reasonable in all the circumstances
      `did it unreasonably fail to follow its notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it unreasonably failed to let you inspect the information, or to give a reasonable explanation, as its notice said
      `did the named provider unreasonably fail to explain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health service provider it named to explain the information unreasonably failed to make, or keep, an arrangement with you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it taken to have refused me access` MEANS
    IF `has it missed a requirement of s 29 or s 34` OR `was the date, time or place unreasonable` OR `did it unreasonably fail to follow its notice` OR `did the named provider unreasonably fail to explain`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 32 -- fees`

DECLARE `What I am charged for` IS ONE OF
    `lodging my request`
    `access to my health information`
    `an explanation of its content under s 29(1)(d)`
    `the review by the health service provider I nominated (s 42)`

-- s 32(1) no fee is required; (2) "A fee may not be charged ... for providing a manner of access to health information
-- (except under section 29(1)(d)) unless a maximum fee has been prescribed for that manner of access"; (2A) "a mental
-- health and wellbeing service provider must not charge a fee to a person for providing access to health information
-- held ... in respect of that person if the information is held ... as a result of providing a mental health and
-- wellbeing service to the person"; (3) not more than the prescribed maximum; (4) the person who explains under s
-- 29(1)(d) "may charge a fee ... that does not exceed the amount of the person's usual fee for a consultation of a
-- comparable duration"; (5) "must not charge for the lodgment of a request". s 42(3): the nominated health service
-- provider "may charge ... a fee (not exceeding the prescribed maximum fee)". The maximum fees are in regulations under s
-- 100, not deposited: an input. 0 means no fee may be charged. A s 42 fee with no maximum prescribed: NOTHING-kind
-- *silent*. The s 32(2A) bar read as reaching access only, not the explainer's s 32(4) fee (fork F4).
@export What is the most I can be charged for this, in dollars (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 32, 42(3))? Lodging a request is free; access may be charged only up to a maximum prescribed for that manner of access (none if none is prescribed), and is free from a mental health and wellbeing service provider for records of your mental health care; an explanation costs at most the explainer's usual consultation fee.
GIVEN `what am I being charged for` IS A `What I am charged for` @desc What the fee is for: lodging your request, access to the information, an explanation of its content (s 29(1)(d)), or the review by the health service provider you nominated after a refusal (s 42)
      `is it my mental health service provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is a mental health and wellbeing service provider holding the information because it gave you a mental health and wellbeing service
      `is a maximum fee prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulations prescribe a maximum fee for this manner of access or service (the Health Records Regulations are not deposited here)
      `the prescribed maximum fee` IS A NUMBER @desc The prescribed maximum fee in dollars, if one is prescribed (any number if not)
      `the explainer's usual consultation fee` IS A NUMBER @desc For an explanation: the explaining practitioner's usual fee for a consultation of comparable length, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most I can be charged` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what am I being charged for`
    WHEN `lodging my request` THEN JUST 0
    WHEN `access to my health information` THEN
        IF `is it my mental health service provider` OR (NOT `is a maximum fee prescribed`)
        THEN JUST 0
        ELSE JUST `the prescribed maximum fee`
    WHEN `an explanation of its content under s 29(1)(d)` THEN JUST `the explainer's usual consultation fee`
    WHEN `the review by the health service provider I nominated (s 42)` THEN
        IF `is a maximum fee prescribed`
        THEN JUST `the prescribed maximum fee`
        ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part 5 Division 3 -- a refusal because access would threaten my own life or health`

-- Situation: only after the organisation has refused access under s 26 because access would seriously threaten your own
-- life or health (s 36 "This Division applies if").
-- s 38(1): "If -- (a) the organisation does not make an offer referred to in section 37; or (b) the individual does not
-- accept an offer ...; or (c) the individual is not satisfied with the outcome of the discussion -- the individual may,
-- within the period specified in subsection (2), give a written notice to the organisation nominating a health service
-- provider". (2) "(a) the period of 21 days after receiving the notice of refusal, if the organisation does not make an
-- offer ... or the individual does not accept such an offer; or (b) the period of 21 days after a discussion". The
-- Division not applying, a discussion not yet held, or a discussion I am satisfied with: no period, NOTHING (s 36 and
-- s 38(1) "if"; a date cannot say no). s 40(2): if a nomination lapses, one more nomination may be made (the Act sets
-- no period for it; finding 5).
@export By when can I nominate a health service provider to review the organisation's refusal of access on the ground that it would seriously threaten my life or health (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 36 to 38)? Within 21 days after receiving the notice of refusal, or, if you accepted its offer of a discussion and are not satisfied, within 21 days after the discussion. The provider must have consented.
GIVEN `was access refused as a serious threat to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation refused you access under s 26 because access would pose a serious threat to your own life or health
      `did it offer a discussion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice of refusal offered a discussion of the information with a suitably qualified health service provider (s 37)
      `did I accept the offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether you accepted it
      `has the discussion been held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you accepted: whether the discussion has taken place
      `am I satisfied with the discussion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it has: whether you are satisfied with its outcome
      `the day I received the refusal` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the notice of refusal (ISO 8601)
      `the day of the discussion` IS A DATE @desc The day of the discussion, if held (ISO 8601; any date if not)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when can I nominate a reviewer` MEANS
    IF NOT `was access refused as a serious threat to me`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF (NOT `did it offer a discussion`) OR (NOT `did I accept the offer`)
         THEN JUST (`the date` 21 `days after` `the day I received the refusal`)
         ELSE IF `has the discussion been held` AND (NOT `am I satisfied with the discussion`)
              THEN JUST (`the date` 21 `days after` `the day of the discussion`)
              ELSE NOTHING

-- s 39(1): the organisation "may, within 14 days after receiving the notice, give a written notice ... stating that the
-- organisation objects" if it "believes on reasonable grounds that the nominated health service provider is not
-- suitable". (2): "Subsection (1) does not apply" where (a) the recorder and the nominee are registered with the same
-- registration board, (b) the nominee is a registered medical practitioner able to understand and interpret the
-- information, or (c) the nominee provides the same kind of service as the recorder and can understand and interpret it.
-- "does not apply", the "if" and the period: JUST FALSE.
@export Can the organisation object to the health service provider I nominated to review its refusal (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 39)? Only by written notice within 14 days after receiving your nomination, only if it believes on reasonable grounds your nominee is unsuitable, and never where your nominee is registered with the same board as the person who recorded the information, is a doctor able to interpret it, or provides the same kind of service and can interpret it. An objection makes the nomination lapse; you may nominate once more.
GIVEN `does it reasonably believe my nominee unsuitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation believes on reasonable grounds that your nominee is not suitable
      `same registration board as the recorder` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your nominee and the practitioner who recorded the information are registered with the same National Board (for example both psychologists)
      `is my nominee a doctor able to interpret it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your nominee is a registered medical practitioner able to understand and interpret the information
      `same kind of service and able to interpret it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your nominee provides the same kind of service as the recorder and can understand and interpret the information
      `the day it received my nomination` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your written nomination (ISO 8601)
      `the day of its objection` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation gave (or would give) its written objection (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the organisation object to my nominee` MEANS
    IF `same registration board as the recorder` OR `is my nominee a doctor able to interpret it` OR `same kind of service and able to interpret it`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `does it reasonably believe my nominee unsuitable` AND (NOT (`the day of its objection` `is after` (`the date` 14 `days after` `the day it received my nomination`)))
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 41: on accepting an offer under s 37(1)(b) or receiving a nomination, "the organisation must give the health
-- information, or a copy of it, to the nominated health service provider within 14 days after receiving the advice or
-- notice unless within that period -- (c) ... the individual withdraws consent ...; or (d) ... the organisation gives a
-- notice under section 39(1) ...; or (e) in any case, the nomination ... lapses." The "unless" met: no duty, NOTHING (a
-- date cannot say no; fork F3).
@export By when must the organisation give my health information to the health service provider I nominated or accepted (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 41)? Within 14 days after it receives your nomination or your acceptance of its offer, unless in that time it validly objects, the nomination lapses, or you withdraw consent to the discussion.
GIVEN `the day it received my nomination or acceptance` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your nomination (s 38) or your acceptance of its offer of a named provider (s 37(1)(b)) (ISO 8601)
      `has it objected in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation gave a valid written objection to your nominee within the 14 days (s 39)
      `has the nomination lapsed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the nomination has lapsed (the nominee died, stopped being a health service provider, or refused or failed to act within a reasonable time)
      `have I withdrawn consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have withdrawn consent to the discussion
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must my nominee receive the information` MEANS
    IF `has it objected in time` OR `has the nomination lapsed` OR `have I withdrawn consent`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the date` 14 `days after` `the day it received my nomination or acceptance`)

§§ `HPP 6.5 to 6.10 -- correcting my health information`

DECLARE `Correction` IS ONE OF
    `the organisation must correct it`
    `the organisation must correct it and keep the incorrect version on a restricted record`
    `the organisation need not correct it`
    `correction only under the Freedom of Information Act 1982`

-- HPP 6.5: "If an organisation holds health information about an individual and the individual is able to establish that
-- the information is inaccurate, incomplete, misleading or not up to date, the organisation must take reasonable steps to
-- correct the information so that it is accurate, complete and up to date but must not delete the information otherwise
-- than in accordance with HPP 4.2." HPP 6.7: where it "accepts the need to correct" but (a) leaving the incorrect
-- information could cause harm or inappropriate care, (b) the form makes correction impossible, or (c) the corrections
-- are so complex or numerous as to risk confusion, it "must place the incorrect information on a record which is not
-- generally available ... and to which access is restricted". s 16: for an FOI agency's document, information "can only
-- be corrected" under the Freedom of Information Act 1982. Not established: the "if" unmet, the duty does not arise. Not
-- held: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Must the organisation correct my health information (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 6.5, 6.7, s 16)? If you can establish that it is inaccurate, incomplete, misleading or out of date, the organisation must take reasonable steps to correct it, without deleting it; where leaving it could cause harm, correction is impossible in that form, or the corrections are many or complex, the incorrect version goes on a restricted record. Victorian government agencies correct under the Freedom of Information Act 1982.
GIVEN `does the organisation hold it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation holds the health information about you
      `can I establish it is wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are able to establish that the information is inaccurate, incomplete, misleading or not up to date
      `is it an FOI agency or Minister's document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a document of a Victorian government agency or a Minister's official document under the Freedom of Information Act 1982
      `does an HPP 6.7 reason apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation considers that leaving the incorrect information, even corrected, could cause you harm or lead to inappropriate care, or the form it is held in makes correction impossible, or the corrections are complex or numerous enough to risk confusion
GIVETH A MAYBE `Correction`
`Must my information be corrected` MEANS
    IF NOT `does the organisation hold it`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `is it an FOI agency or Minister's document`
         THEN JUST `correction only under the Freedom of Information Act 1982`
         ELSE IF NOT `can I establish it is wrong`
              THEN JUST `the organisation need not correct it`
              ELSE IF `does an HPP 6.7 reason apply`
                   THEN JUST `the organisation must correct it and keep the incorrect version on a restricted record`
                   ELSE JUST `the organisation must correct it`

-- HPP 6.6: "If -- (a) the organisation is not willing to correct the health information in accordance with a request by
-- the individual; and (b) no decision or recommendation to the effect that the information should be corrected ... is
-- pending or has been made under this Act or any other law; and (c) the individual gives to the organisation a written
-- statement concerning the requested correction -- the organisation must take reasonable steps to associate the
-- statement with the information." The "if": JUST FALSE.
@export Must the organisation attach my written statement to my health information when it will not correct it (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 6.6)? Yes, if it is not willing to make the correction you asked for, no decision or recommendation that it be corrected is pending or made, and you give it a written statement about the correction.
GIVEN `is it unwilling to correct it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is not willing to correct the information as you asked
      `is a correction decision pending or made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a decision or recommendation that the information be corrected, wholly or partly as you asked, is pending or has been made (for example by the Health Complaints Commissioner or the Tribunal)
      `have I given a written statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the organisation a written statement about the correction you asked for
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my statement be attached` MEANS
    IF `is it unwilling to correct it` AND (NOT `is a correction decision pending or made`) AND `have I given a written statement`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

@export Has the organisation decided my correction request in time (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 6.9)? It must take reasonable steps to tell you its decision as soon as practicable, and not later than 30 days after it received the request. Answers done in time, not done in time, or still open.
GIVEN `the day it received my correction request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your request to correct your health information (ISO 8601)
      `has it told me its decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has notified you of its decision
      `the day it told me` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day (ISO 8601; any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `What became of the duty`
`Has my correction request been decided in time` MEANS
    `what became of the organisation's duty` (`HPP 6.9 -- the organisation must notify me of its correction decision` (`the days from` `the day it received my correction request` `to` (`HPP 6.9 -- the last day to notify the decision` `the day it received my correction request`))) `notify me of its decision on my correction request` `has it told me its decision` (`the days from` `the day it received my correction request` `to` `the day it told me`) (`the days from` `the day it received my correction request` `to` `today`)

-- HPP 6.10: "An organisation must provide written reasons for refusal of access or a refusal to correct health
-- information." s 34(2)(a) the same for access. No refusal: NOTHING-kind *silent*.
@export Must the organisation give me written reasons (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 6.10, s 34(2)(a))? Yes, for any refusal of access or refusal to correct your health information.
GIVEN `has it refused access or correction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has refused you access, or refused to correct your health information
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be given written reasons` MEANS
    IF `has it refused access or correction` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

-- HPP 5.2: "On request by an individual, an organisation must take reasonable steps -- (a) to let the individual know --
-- (i) whether the organisation holds health information relating to the individual; and (ii) the steps that the
-- individual should take ... to obtain access ...; and (b) if the organisation holds health information ..., to let the
-- individual know in general terms" its nature, purposes, and how it is collected, held, used and disclosed. s 16: nothing
-- in HPP 5.2 applies to an FOI agency's documents (JUST FALSE, the flat "nothing ... applies"). No request yet: "On
-- request" read as a condition, JUST FALSE (fork F5).
@export Must the organisation tell me whether it holds health information about me, and how to get it (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 5.2)? On your request it must take reasonable steps to tell you whether it holds any, what steps to take for access, and in general terms what it is, why it is used, and how it is collected, held, used and disclosed.
GIVEN `have I asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the organisation
      `is it an FOI agency or Minister's document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information would be in a document of a Victorian government agency or a Minister under the Freedom of Information Act 1982 (that Act applies instead)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must it tell me what it holds` MEANS
    IF `is it an FOI agency or Minister's document`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `have I asked` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `HPP 1, 2, 4 and 11 -- collection, use, disclosure, keeping and transfer`

DECLARE `Collection ground` IS ONE OF
    `no collection ground`
    `I consented (HPP 1.1(a))`
    `required, authorised or permitted by law (HPP 1.1(b))`
    `needed for my health service while I cannot consent and no representative can be asked (HPP 1.1(c))`
    `disclosed to it under HPP 2.2(a), (f), (i) or (l) or HPP 2.5 (HPP 1.1(d))`
    `research or statistics in the public interest under the guidelines (HPP 1.1(e))`
    `to prevent or lessen a serious threat (HPP 1.1(f))`
    `by a law enforcement agency for a law enforcement function (HPP 1.1(g))`
    `necessary for a legal or equitable claim (HPP 1.1(h))`
    `prescribed circumstances (HPP 1.1(i))`

-- HPP 1.1: "An organisation must not collect health information about an individual unless the information is necessary
-- for one or more of its functions or activities and at least one of the following applies" -- (a) to (i). "must not ...
-- unless": JUST FALSE when no ground is met. HPP 1.2 to 1.5 (fair means, from the individual where practicable, notice of
-- the matters in 1.4) are further duties, not conditions of this answer. s 20(1): HPP 1 applies only to information
-- collected after 1 July 2002.
@export May the organisation collect my health information (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 1.1)? Only if it is necessary for its functions or activities and a ground applies: your consent, the law, your health care while you cannot consent, a permitted disclosure to it, approved research, a serious threat, a law enforcement function, a legal or equitable claim, or prescribed circumstances.
GIVEN `is it necessary for its functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information is necessary for one or more of the organisation's functions or activities
      `which ground applies` IS A `Collection ground` @desc Which HPP 1.1 ground applies, with all of its conditions met (choose the "no ... ground" value if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May it collect my health information` MEANS
    IF `is it necessary for its functions` AND (NOT (`which ground applies` EQUALS `no collection ground`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Secondary purpose ground` IS ONE OF
    `no secondary purpose ground`
    `directly related and I would reasonably expect it (HPP 2.2(a))`
    `I consented (HPP 2.2(b))`
    `required, authorised or permitted by law (HPP 2.2(c))`
    `needed for my health service while I cannot consent (HPP 2.2(d))`
    `my provider's further safe and effective care, under the guidelines (HPP 2.2(e))`
    `funding, management, planning or training, de-identified or impracticable to ask (HPP 2.2(f))`
    `research or statistics in the public interest under the guidelines (HPP 2.2(g))`
    `to lessen or prevent a serious threat (HPP 2.2(h))`
    `investigating or reporting suspected unlawful activity (HPP 2.2(i))`
    `a law enforcement function (HPP 2.2(j))`
    `necessary for a legal or equitable claim (HPP 2.2(k))`
    `prescribed circumstances (HPP 2.2(l))`

-- HPP 2.1: "An organisation may use or disclose health information about an individual for the primary purpose for which
-- the information was collected". HPP 2.2: it "must not use or disclose health information about an individual for a
-- purpose (the secondary purpose) other than the primary purpose ... unless at least one of the following paragraphs
-- applies" -- (a) to (l). The note: "Nothing in HPP 2 requires an organisation to disclose". "must not ... unless": JUST
-- FALSE when no ground is met. HPP 2.3: a written note of a disclosure under (i) or (j). HPP 2.4 is the next question.
@export May the organisation use or disclose my health information for this purpose (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 2.1, 2.2)? It may for the primary purpose it was collected for; for any other purpose only on a listed ground, such as a directly related purpose you would expect, your consent, the law, a serious threat, law enforcement, or the establishment, exercise or defence of a legal or equitable claim.
GIVEN `is it the primary purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the use or disclosure is for the primary purpose for which the information was collected
      `which ground applies` IS A `Secondary purpose ground` @desc For any other purpose: which HPP 2.2 ground applies, with all of its conditions met (choose the "no ... ground" value if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May it use or disclose my health information` MEANS
    IF `is it the primary purpose` OR (NOT (`which ground applies` EQUALS `no secondary purpose ground`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- HPP 2.4: "Despite HPP 2.2, a health service provider may disclose health information about an individual to an
-- immediate family member of the individual if -- (a) either -- (i) the disclosure is necessary to provide appropriate
-- health services to or care of the individual; or (ii) the disclosure is made for compassionate reasons; and (b) the
-- disclosure is limited to the extent reasonable and necessary ...; and (c) the individual is incapable of giving
-- consent ... within the meaning of section 85(3); and (d) the disclosure is not contrary to any wish -- (i) expressed by
-- the individual before the individual became incapable ... and not changed or withdrawn ...; and (ii) of which the
-- organisation is aware or could be made aware by taking reasonable steps; and (e) in the case of an immediate family
-- member who is under the age of 18 years ... the immediate family member has sufficient maturity". The "if": JUST
-- FALSE. Not a health service provider, or not an immediate family member: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*
-- (HPP 2.2 may still allow it).
@export May my health service provider tell a member of my family about my health while I cannot consent (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 2.4)? It may disclose to an immediate family member (parent, child, sibling, spouse or domestic partner, a relative in your household, or someone you nominated) if it is needed for your care or for compassionate reasons, limited to what is reasonable, you cannot consent, it is not against a wish you expressed that it knows or could find out, and a family member under 18 is mature enough.
GIVEN `is the discloser a health service provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person or body disclosing is a health service provider
      `who is the family member to me` IS A `Relationship to me` @desc How the person told is related to you: parent, child, sibling, spouse or domestic partner, a relative (grandparent, grandchild, uncle, aunt, nephew or niece) living in your household, a relative not in your household, a person you nominated to the provider, or someone else
      `is it for my care or compassionate reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the disclosure is necessary for appropriate health services to or care of you, or is made for compassionate reasons
      `is it limited to what is reasonable and necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the disclosure is limited to the extent reasonable and necessary for that purpose
      `am I incapable of consenting` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you cannot understand or communicate a consent, because of age, injury, illness, disability, impairment or mental disorder, even with reasonable help
      `is it against a wish I expressed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the disclosure is contrary to a wish you expressed before becoming incapable, not changed or withdrawn, that the provider knows of or could find out by reasonable steps
      `is the family member under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the family member is under 18
      `are they mature enough` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If under 18: whether, in the circumstances, they have sufficient maturity to receive the information
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May my provider tell my family member` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is the discloser a health service provider`) OR (NOT (`s 3 -- an immediate family member` `who is the family member to me`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF     `is it for my care or compassionate reasons`
            AND `is it limited to what is reasonable and necessary`
            AND `am I incapable of consenting`
            AND (NOT `is it against a wish I expressed`)
            AND ((NOT `is the family member under 18`) OR `are they mature enough`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- HPP 4.2: "A health service provider must not delete health information relating to an individual, even if it is later
-- found or claimed to be inaccurate, unless -- (a) the deletion is permitted, authorised or required by the regulations
-- or any other law; or (b) the deletion is not contrary to the regulations or any other law and occurs -- (i) in the case
-- of health information collected while the individual was a child, after the individual attains the age of 25 years; or
-- (ii) in any case, more than 7 years after the last occasion on which a health service was provided to the individual
-- by the provider -- whichever is the later." The first day deletion may occur: for (ii) the day after the day 7 years
-- (84 months) after the last service ("more than"); for (i) the 25th birthday (300 months after birth; "after ...
-- attains", fork F6), and the later of the two. Where a regulation or other law permits earlier deletion, or bars it,
-- the Act gives no day: NOTHING-kind *points outside the sources* (s 100(1)(d) regulations may change the periods; not
-- deposited).
@export What is the earliest day my health service provider may delete my health records (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 4.2)? Unless another law or the regulations say otherwise, not until more than 7 years after it last gave you a health service, and, for records made while you were a child, not before you turn 25, whichever is later. Useful for knowing how long records for a claim must be kept.
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth (ISO 8601)
      `was any of it collected while I was a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of the information was collected while you were under 18
      `the last day it gave me a health service` IS A DATE @desc The last day this provider gave you a health service (ISO 8601)
      `does another law or regulation govern deletion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a regulation or another law permits, authorises or requires deletion, or forbids it (for example public records law for a public hospital); usually no
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`When may my records be deleted` MEANS
    IF `does another law or regulation govern deletion`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `was any of it collected while I was a child`
         THEN JUST (`the later of` `the 25th birthday` `and` `the day after 7 years`)
         ELSE JUST `the day after 7 years`
    WHERE
        `the 25th birthday` MEANS `the date` 300 `months after` `my date of birth`
        `the day after 7 years` MEANS `the date` 1 `days after` (`the date` 84 `months after` `the last day it gave me a health service`)

-- HPP 11.1: "If an individual -- (a) requests a health service provider to make health information relating to the
-- individual held by the provider available to another health service provider; or (b) authorises another health service
-- provider to request ... -- a health service provider to whom the request is made and who holds health information about
-- the individual must, on payment of a fee not exceeding the prescribed maximum fee and subject to the regulations,
-- provide a copy or written summary of that health information to that other health service provider." HPP 11.2: "as
-- soon as practicable". The "if" (and "on payment") unmet: JUST FALSE.
@export Must my health service provider send a copy or summary of my records to another health service provider (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) HPP 11)? Yes, as soon as practicable, if you asked it to, or authorised the other provider to ask, and any fee charged (at most the prescribed maximum) is paid.
GIVEN `does it hold my health information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health service provider holds health information about you
      `have I asked or authorised the request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked it to make the information available to another health service provider, or authorised the other provider to ask
      `is any fee charged paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any fee it charges (not more than the prescribed maximum) has been paid; answer yes if it charges none
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my records be sent to my new provider` MEANS
    IF `does it hold my health information` AND `have I asked or authorised the request` AND `is any fee charged paid`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Part 6 -- complaints and the Tribunal`

-- Situation: something has gone wrong with the handling of your health information, or with your request for access.
-- s 18: "an act or practice of an organisation is an interference with the privacy of an individual if, and only if --
-- (a) the act or practice breaches Part 5 or a Health Privacy Principle in relation to health information that relates
-- to the individual; or (b) the act or practice breaches HPP 7 in relation to an identifier; or (c) the act or practice
-- is or results in a failure to provide access to health information that relates to the individual in accordance with
-- Part 5 or HPP 6." "if, and only if": TRUE or FALSE.
@export Is what the organisation did an "interference with my privacy" (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 18)? It is if, and only if, it breached Part 5 or a Health Privacy Principle about your health information, breached HPP 7 about an identifier, or failed to give you access under Part 5 or HPP 6. Only an interference can be complained of.
GIVEN `did it breach Part 5 or an HPP` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or practice breached Part 5 (access) or a Health Privacy Principle in relation to your health information
      `did it breach HPP 7 about an identifier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it breached HPP 7 (identifiers) in relation to an identifier
      `was it a failure to give me access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is, or results in, a failure to give you access to your health information as Part 5 or HPP 6 requires
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is it an interference with my privacy` MEANS
    `did it breach Part 5 or an HPP` OR `did it breach HPP 7 about an identifier` OR `was it a failure to give me access`

DECLARE `Who complains` IS ONE OF
    `me`
    `my parent`
    `a person chosen by me or my parent`
    `a person the Commissioner considers has a sufficient interest`
    `a person I authorised to complain for me`
    `the legal representative of my estate`
    `someone else`

-- s 45(1): "An individual may complain to the Health Complaints Commissioner about an act or practice that may be an
-- interference with the privacy of the individual." (4) "A complaint must be in writing". s 47(1): by a child, or on a
-- child's behalf by a parent, a person chosen by the child or a parent, or a person the Commissioner considers has a
-- sufficient interest; (3): for an individual "unable to complain by reason of injury, disease, senility, illness,
-- disability, physical impairment or mental disorder", by a person they authorised or, if they cannot authorise anyone,
-- by a person the Commissioner considers has a sufficient interest. s 95(2): after death, by the legal representative.
-- s 20(6): an act before 1 July 2002 "does not constitute an interference ... about which a complaint may be made"
-- (JUST FALSE). Not in writing: a bare "must" not done, JUST FALSE (fork F7). Nothing that may be an interference, or a
-- person outside the lists: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Can this complaint be made to the Health Complaints Commissioner about the handling of my health information (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 45, 47, 95)? You may complain in writing about an act or practice that may be an interference with your privacy. For a child, a parent or a person chosen by the child or parent may complain; for a person unable to complain because of illness or disability, a person they authorised; after death, the executor or administrator.
GIVEN `may it be an interference with my privacy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or practice may be an interference with your privacy (the s 18 question answers this)
      `did it happen before 1 July 2002` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or practice happened before 1 July 2002
      `who is complaining` IS A `Who complains` @desc Who makes the complaint: you, your parent, a person chosen by you or your parent, a person the Commissioner considers has a sufficient interest, a person you authorised, the executor or administrator of your estate, or someone else
      `am I a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under 18
      `am I unable to complain myself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are unable to complain because of injury, disease, senility, illness, disability, physical impairment or mental disorder
      `am I unable to authorise anyone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether you are also unable to authorise another person to complain for you
      `have I died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the person the information is about) have died
      `is it in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is in writing, lodged by hand, fax, email or post
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this complaint be made` MEANS
    IF NOT `may it be an interference with my privacy`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `did it happen before 1 July 2002` OR (NOT `is it in writing`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `have I died`
              THEN CONSIDER `who is complaining`
                   WHEN `the legal representative of my estate` THEN JUST TRUE
                   OTHERWISE NOTHING
              ELSE CONSIDER `who is complaining`
                   WHEN `me` THEN JUST TRUE
                   WHEN `my parent` THEN IF `am I a child` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
                   WHEN `a person chosen by me or my parent` THEN IF `am I a child` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
                   WHEN `a person the Commissioner considers has a sufficient interest` THEN IF `am I a child` OR (`am I unable to complain myself` AND `am I unable to authorise anyone`) THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
                   WHEN `a person I authorised to complain for me` THEN IF `am I unable to complain myself` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
                   OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 51(1)(d): the Commissioner may decline to entertain a complaint, by notice within 90 days after lodgment, if "the
-- complaint ... was made more than 12 months after the complainant became aware of the act or practice". The last day
-- that is not "more than 12 months after" is the day 12 months after awareness (fork F2 for short months).
@export By when should I complain to the Health Complaints Commissioner so that my complaint cannot be declined as late (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 51(1)(d))? The Commissioner may decline a complaint made more than 12 months after you became aware of the act or practice; this is the last day that is not.
GIVEN `the day I became aware` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware of the act or practice you complain about (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when should I complain` MEANS `the date` 12 `months after` `the day I became aware`

@export Can the Health Complaints Commissioner decline my complaint because I made it too late (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 51(1)(d))? The Commissioner may decline it, by written notice within 90 days after you lodged it, if you complained more than 12 months after becoming aware of the act or practice. You may then require referral to the Tribunal within 60 days (s 51(5)).
GIVEN `the day I became aware` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware of the act or practice (ISO 8601)
      `the day I lodged the complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged the complaint with the Commissioner (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my complaint be declined as late` MEANS
    IF `the day I lodged the complaint` `is after` (`the date` 12 `months after` `the day I became aware`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Commissioner's notice` IS ONE OF
    `a notice declining to entertain my complaint (s 51)`
    `a notice of the Commissioner's decision on my accepted complaint (s 56(4))`
    `a notice after conciliation failed (s 63(1))`
    `a notice of the Commissioner's ruling (s 64(2))`
    `a notice that the respondent has not complied or reported (s 64(8))`

-- ss 51(5), 57(1), 63(3), (4), 65(1): "Within 60 days after receiving" the notice the complainant "may require" the
-- Commissioner to refer the complaint to the Tribunal; s 65(2): "within 30 days after receipt of a notice under section
-- 64(8)" where the respondent failed to comply or to report. s 57(1) is read on every s 56(4) notice, as written (finding
-- 3). The Commissioner "must comply" (ss 51(6), 57(2), 63(5), 65(3)); missing the period lets the Commissioner dismiss
-- the complaint, after which "A complainant may take no further action under this Act" (ss 51(9), 57(5), 63(9)).
@export By when can I require the Health Complaints Commissioner to refer my complaint to the Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 51(5), 57(1), 63(3), (4), 65(1), (2))? Within 60 days after receiving the Commissioner's notice declining the complaint, deciding on it, after failed conciliation, or of a ruling; within 30 days after a notice that the respondent has not complied with a ruling or reported. Miss it and the complaint may be dismissed for good.
GIVEN `which notice did I receive` IS A `Commissioner's notice` @desc Which notice from the Commissioner you received: declining to entertain the complaint (s 51), the decision on an accepted complaint (s 56(4)), after conciliation failed (s 63(1)), the ruling (s 64(2)), or that the respondent did not comply with the ruling or report (s 64(8))
      `the day I received it` IS A DATE @desc The day you received that notice (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can I require referral to the Tribunal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `which notice did I receive`
    WHEN `a notice that the respondent has not complied or reported (s 64(8))` THEN `the date` 30 `days after` `the day I received it`
    OTHERWISE `the date` 60 `days after` `the day I received it`

-- s 78(1)(a)(iv): the Tribunal may order "that the complainant is entitled to a specified amount, not exceeding $100 000,
-- by way of compensation for any loss or damage suffered by the complainant, including injury to the complainant's
-- feelings or humiliation". The closing words of s 78(1) add reimbursement of "expenses reasonably incurred" in making the
-- complaint and the proceedings, with no cap. s 8(1): the Act gives no civil cause of action otherwise than by its own
-- procedures (finding 1).
@export What is the most compensation the Tribunal can order for my proven privacy complaint, in dollars (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) s 78(1)(a)(iv))? The Tribunal may order compensation for loss or damage, including hurt feelings and humiliation, of not more than $100,000; your reasonable expenses of the complaint and proceedings may be ordered on top.
GIVEN `the loss or damage I claim` IS A NUMBER @desc The loss or damage you claim, in dollars, including for injury to feelings or humiliation (excluding your expenses of the complaint)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most compensation I can be awarded` MEANS
    min `the loss or damage I claim` 100000

DECLARE `Conduct` IS ONE OF
    `requiring a consent by threat, intimidation or false representation (s 80)`
    `destroying or damaging health information to evade the Act (s 81(1))`
    `removing health information from Victoria to evade the Act (s 81(2))`
    `requesting or obtaining access by threat, intimidation or false representation (s 82)`
    `persuading me not to request access or complain, or to withdraw (s 83)`

-- ss 80 to 83, each: "In the case of a body corporate, 300 penalty units; In any other case, 60 penalty units." s 82
-- reaches anyone who obtains access "to health information relating to himself, herself or any other person", so the
-- individual too. s 83 needs "threat, intimidation or false representation"; s 79 "false representation". s 94: a
-- prosecution is brought by police, the Commissioner or a person the Commissioner authorises.
@export What is the most a person or body can be fined, in penalty units, for this conduct about my health information (Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) ss 80 to 83)? Forcing a consent, destroying or removing health information to evade the Act, getting access by threats or lies, and pressuring you by threat, intimidation or false representation to drop an access request or complaint are offences: 300 penalty units for a company, 60 for anyone else.
GIVEN `what did they do` IS A `Conduct` @desc The conduct: requiring a consent by threat, intimidation or false representation (s 80); destroying, defacing or damaging health information to evade the Act (s 81(1)); removing it from Victoria to evade the Act (s 81(2)); requesting or obtaining access by threat, intimidation or false representation (s 82); or persuading you by threat, intimidation or false representation not to make or pursue an access request or complaint, or to withdraw it (s 83)
      `is it a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender is a body corporate (a company)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most they can be fined` MEANS
    IF `is it a body corporate` THEN 300 ELSE 60
